#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/scripts

# Server-side TLS termination, minimal. Run it directly -- nothing here
# blocks, so the trace completes and hands the shell straight back while the
# gate keeps running. The server outlives this script because the ENTITY
# holds the accept scope, not because the trace is still executing.
#
# For the full site over TLS -- landing page, file tree, chess and forum
# lobbies -- see run_tls_website.etcs. This file is the smoke test: the
# smallest trace that proves termination works, plus the checks worth
# running against it.
#
# The point of this trace is what it DOESN'T say. There is exactly one TLS
# line in it. The page, the tree and the routing are byte-identical to
# run_website.etcs -- HttpServer::Serve and every other subscriber keep
# receiving plaintext and never learn a handshake happened. If serving a
# page over TLS required teaching the page about TLS, the change would be
# in the wrong layer.
#
# Generate a self-signed cert first. The -addext line is not optional
# padding: without a subjectAltName, modern clients reject the cert on
# hostname grounds ALONE, before ever considering the issuer -- which would
# make check 3 below pass for the wrong reason.
#
#   openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -days 30 \
#     -keyout /tmp/etcs-key.pem -out /tmp/etcs-cert.pem \
#     -subj "/CN=localhost" \
#     -addext "subjectAltName=DNS:localhost,IP:127.0.0.1"
#
# OpenSSL 3.x writes the key as PKCS#8 ("BEGIN PRIVATE KEY"), which
# mbedtls_pk_parse_keyfile reads without help.

NetworkProvider
HttpServer web
spawn web
SetPort 8443

# BEFORE Start, and that is load-bearing rather than stylistic. Start mints
# the ConnectionManager and Opens it in one step, and Open begins accepting
# immediately -- so there is no later point in this trace at which the gate
# exists but has not yet taken a connection. Enabling TLS afterwards would
# always race the first client. This line only stores the paths; Start is
# what hands them to the gate, and Start REFUSES to come up if either file
# fails to load rather than falling back to plaintext on a port that was
# asked to be TLS.
EnableTLS /tmp/etcs-cert.pem /tmp/etcs-key.pem

web.add(StaticHtmlPage landing)
SetHtmlFromFile ./www/index.html
context NetworkProvider::HttpServer web

web.add(FileHtmlPage tree)
LoadFromDisk ./www
ListPaths tree
context NetworkProvider::HttpServer web

Start
IsStarted

# NO Stop/Delete here, deliberately. A trace that starts a server and then
# stops it in the same breath has served nothing -- the gate would be gone
# before any client could reach it. Stop it from the shell when you are
# done:
#
#   context NetworkProvider::HttpServer web
#   Stop
#
# https://localhost:8443/     (note: https, and 8443 -- not 8080)
#
# ── What to check, in order of what it proves ────────────────────────────
#
# 1. curl -k https://localhost:8443/
#    Serves the page. Handshake terminated in ConnectionManager::onConnection
#    before any subscriber was dispatched to.
#
# 2. curl -k https://localhost:8443/ https://localhost:8443/ -v
#    THE interesting one for this change. Two requests, ONE handshake --
#    curl should report the connection reused. This exercises the
#    finalizeIfDraining / RecycleForNextRequest split: the TLS session must
#    survive a recycle for the same peer and only be freed when the
#    connection returns to the pool for a different one. A second handshake
#    here means Free() is being reached on the recycle path.
#
# 3. curl https://localhost:8443/          (no -k)
#    Rejected for an untrusted ISSUER -- read the error and confirm that is
#    what it says. A hostname-mismatch error instead would mean the SAN
#    above did not make it into the cert. Either way a rejection proves a
#    real chain was actually presented.
#
# 4. curl http://localhost:8443/
#    Plaintext into a TLS gate must fail the handshake and drop the
#    connection. It must NOT serve a page.
#
# 5. list NetworkProvider::SocketConnectionState
#    Run it before and after a few clients. The count returns to its resting
#    pool size. A count that only grows is a reference that was never
#    released -- the exact failure the io_inflight_ discipline exists to
#    catch, and the one a TLS handshake adds new ways to reach.
#
# 6. Stop (from the shell, as above)
#    Should return promptly. This is the path that exposed the close/cancel
#    race: the accept window has to actually retire, and it only can if the
#    listening descriptor outlives the cancel that names it.
